= Threats to Validity <sec:threats>

_Construct validity:_ Evaluating natural language outputs via an LLM-as-a-Judge could introduce evaluator biases. To mitigate this threat, the judge (GPT-5.6 Luna) was completely isolated from the models under test, configured with deterministic temperature ($T=0.0$), and governed by formally discretized rubrics ($s in {0, 1, 2, 3}$). Furthermore, numerical dimensions (ALTUS maturity scoring and AHP transitive consistency $"CR"$) were evaluated using direct mathematical metrics without language model intervention.

_Internal validity:_ The risk of parameter overfitting was neutralized through strict partitioning: pilot case `G1` was reserved to calibrate the semantic threshold ($tau^* = 0.35$), while the remaining three cases were evaluated blindly. Furthermore, the SIPAC evaluation employed _Teacher Forcing_, isolating each deductive stage with reference data; while this isolates the intrinsic competence of each node without compounding upstream errors, it assumes as a limitation the inability to audit cumulative cascading failure propagation across an end-to-end autonomous run.

_External validity and computational viability:_ Although the benchmark is grounded in four real-world organizations with comprehensive field consulting dossiers, all belong to the small and medium-sized enterprise (SME) segment, introducing a structural bias toward lower digital maturity levels ($L_1$–$L_2$). While this accurately mirrors the predominant business landscape, generalization to large multinational corporations requires further validation. Regarding operational viability on consumer hardware without dedicated infrastructure (MacBook Air M4, 16 GB RAM), context accumulation in ALTUS saturated unified memory for models $>= 7"B"$ (~4.8 hours due to swap degradation), whereas compact 4B architectures (Gemma 4 E4B, 67 unattended minutes) proved to be the optimal configuration for private, sovereign local audits in SMEs without risking sensitive data leakage.

_Conclusion validity:_ The observed inter-model differences and the contrast against direct prompting are supported by closed formal metrics (statistically consistent MAE reduction, $"CR" < 0.10$ ratios, and Hungarian algorithm bipartite matches), ensuring the quantitative reproducibility of the findings.
